# 🧩 Outside the Box

That is your theme. Build one complete **puzzle game** around it. What kind of puzzle,
and everything around it, is yours to invent: this is the most open-ended of briefs,
with no specification and no reference design to match.

The name is the puzzle-solver's creed: *think outside the box.* A good puzzle asks the
player to see past the obvious and find the insight hiding in plain sight — and a good
puzzle *game* is a designer setting that moment up on purpose, again and again. That is
what you are building.

Only a few things are fixed, and your game must deliver all of them:

- **It is a puzzle game.** The core of play is a challenge the player solves by
  *thinking*, reaching an insight, a plan, a solution, rather than by reflexes or
  chance. What that challenge is is entirely up to you: spatial, logical, mechanical,
  physical, wordplay, anything that makes a player stop and work it out.
- **At least eight levels.** Ship a minimum of eight distinct, hand-designed levels, or
  puzzles, stages, boards, whatever your game calls them. This is a floor, not a
  target: build more if they are good, but never fewer.
- **A real difficulty ramp.** Those levels must climb. Start gentle and teaching, and
  build to genuinely hard, each level asking a little more than the one before. You do
  not have to show the player a difficulty rating, but the order they play in must be
  a deliberate progression, and getting that ordering right (predicting how hard each
  puzzle actually is) is a real part of the challenge. A player should feel the
  difficulty rise, never lurch backward.

Beyond those, everything is open: the puzzle mechanic and how it works, the setting and
its art, whether there is any story, how the player moves between levels, and what
winning a level and winning the game look like. Invent the puzzle you find most
interesting, and design the levels that show it off, from its simplest form to its
most demanding.

"Outside the Box" is the name of the jam, **not** the name of your game. Title the
game yourself, to fit the game you build.

Your project root is `{{workspace}}`. Build the game there.

## Your time budget

You have **about {{time_limit_hours}} hours** of wall-clock time for this run. The
session is stopped at that point, and whatever is committed by then is what gets
published. Budget the time so you can build the assets, verify the game, and commit
before the clock runs out. Running `date` in the container reports the current time,
so you can see how much of the budget remains. Scope to finish comfortably rather
than racing the deadline.

## Your starting point

A starter project is already in place at `{{workspace}}`: a `package.json` that pins
Playwright as a dev dependency, with its dependencies already installed.
`node_modules/` is populated and headless Chromium is downloaded, so you can build
and verify right away. Build on this project: keep the `package.json`, add your
framework, bundler, and a `build` script on top of it, and keep the lockfile in sync.

## Producing the game's assets

The game ships its own art, animation, effects, and sound, and you must produce them
for real. Placeholder rectangles, stand-ins drawn at runtime, or silence count as
unfinished work. Six asset-generation binaries are on your `PATH` to help. Run each
with `--help` to learn its operations:

- `draw`, `draw-sheet`: 2D sprites and animated sprite sheets.
- `particle-2d`: 2D particle effects.
- `sfx-synth`, `sfx-sample`: sound effects, synthesized or sample-based.
- `music`: music tracks.

Use them, or produce assets any other way you prefer. These binaries are on your
`PATH` only while this run is live, not when your build is re-run to validate it, so
asset generation is a one-time step. Generate the assets once into committed files
and have `npm run build` bundle them; it must not invoke `draw` or any other
generation binary.

## The fixed build/serve interface

The project stays a Node project built with only Node.js and npm-installed
dependencies. Extend the provided `package.json` and commit a `package-lock.json`,
running `npm install` after changing dependencies so the lockfile matches. `npm ci`
followed by `npm run build`, with no other manual step, must produce the static site
into one of `dist/`, `build/`, or `out/` at the project root, with an `index.html` at
the root of that directory. Serving that directory as-is must run the game.

The build must also work at **any base path**. It is played back from a per-run
sub-path, so every URL it constructs at runtime, including every produced asset, must
be page-relative and never start with `/`. For a bundler, set a relative base such as
`base: './'`.

You can write your game's core logic in Rust and compile it to WebAssembly if you
prefer: this container has the Rust toolchain, the `wasm32-unknown-unknown` target,
and `wasm-bindgen`/`wasm-pack` on `PATH`. Those tools are on `PATH` only while this
run is live, so the compiled wasm is a build input rather than a build step. Compile
it once during the run, commit the resulting `.wasm` and any generated JS glue, and
have `npm run build` bundle the committed output without invoking `cargo` or
`wasm-pack`. Plain JavaScript or TypeScript is fine too; reach for wasm only if it
genuinely helps your game.

## Verifying your work

Playwright and a headless Chromium are installed in the project as dependencies, and
the container has network access, so you can build the game, serve it, open it in a
browser, and play it. There is no global Playwright on `PATH`; the project's own is
reached by importing it (`import { chromium } from "playwright"`) or by running
`npx playwright`. This container has no Chromium sandbox, so Chromium has to be
launched with the `--no-sandbox` argument.

What is judged is the static production build produced from your committed source,
served as-is. Nothing is rebuilt, repaired, or played by anyone else between the end
of this run and that judgement.

## Commit your work

Your project root is a git repository, initialized before you started. Your finished
implementation is published directly from this repository's commits, so uncommitted
work is **not** published. When the game is complete and verified, run `git add -A`
then `git commit`, and confirm `git status` reports a clean working tree. A
`.gitignore` already excludes `node_modules/` and the build output, so a clean tree
means you have committed your source, your `package-lock.json`, a short `README.md`,
and the produced assets the game ships.
